<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuvalu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tuvalu is an archipelagic country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> midway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, made up of three reef islands and six atolls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> were its first inhabitants, spreading out from Samoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. Álvaro de Mendaña was the first European known to sail through the archipelago, in 1568. A British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was proclaimed in 1892, and the islands joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> Colony in 1916. After a referendum in 1974 the colony was divided, and Tuvalu became a separate colony on 1 January 1976. It became fully independent on 1 October 1978.</p></div>
